+## Mode 6 (IGR disabled)
+
+When per-game Compatibility Mode 6 disables IGR, the RA build keeps the in-game snapshot tick through a dedicated VBLANK handler, but only when telemetry successfully initialized. It does not install an IGR worker or pad-open hooks. Achievements can still be recorded; controller IGR and client-triggered **RESET CONSOLE** are unavailable. The physical console buttons retain their normal hardware behavior. Mode 6 does not cure incompatibility with games that reject a VBLANK handler itself. This behavior follows xeRAbora alpha.17; console compatibility varies by game and launch transport.
+
+## RA Mode: Xerabora or Caduceus
+
+In the **RA build**, open **Settings → Network → RA Mode** and choose **Xerabora** (the default) or **Caduceus**. Save settings to retain the choice. Old configurations without `ra_mode` keep Xerabora. The normal build has no RA mode row or telemetry code.
+
+This selector chooses the PC service protocol; it does not enable telemetry by itself. Keep **RA Telemetry** enabled when you want tracking. No PC application or server binaries are bundled by this change; the companion service is managed separately through PS2-Servers.
+
+| Mode | Menu and launch behavior |
+|---|---|
+| Xerabora | Existing xeRAbora discovery, watch-list transfer and in-game telemetry |
+| Caduceus | Signed-in session checks, compatible watch lists/telemetry, account and game achievement browsing, and in-game gold-pulse notices by default; experimental title/points cards are an explicit opt-in |
+
+Caduceus adds a UDP **18197** catalog/session bridge (`CADQ2` / `CADR2`). Once a ready bridge is found, RiptOPL requests watch lists from the engine on that same PC at UDP **18194**. For local-device launches, the in-game module repeats bridge discovery **only against the configured SMB host IPv4 address** before contacting that PC's engine; a standalone Xerabora reply or a faster responder elsewhere on the LAN cannot substitute for the Caduceus bridge. Xerabora mode keeps automatic broadcast discovery. UDP **18195** is the console's telemetry/notice port, and UDP **18196** is its menu reply port. Permit these connections in the companion PC's firewall. Run one intended achievement service per host to avoid port conflicts. By default, Caduceus's pairing bridge uses the configured SMB server IPv4 address, never the fastest LAN responder. **Network → RA companion IP** can override it independently of the SMB and HTTP game servers; set **0.0.0.0** to retain the legacy fallback. This host is used by Caduceus pairing, support checks, link tests, in-game discovery and POPStarter. When explicitly set, Xerabora is also probed by unicast, not broadcast. A NetBIOS-only SMB address without an IPv4 fallback requires the separate RA host setting.
+
+Every tracked Caduceus launch rechecks its session. If the bridge is offline or unavailable, the game launches normally without achievement telemetry. If Caduceus reports **OFFLINE**, sign in through the companion service and retry. RiptOPL never stores your RetroAchievements password or API key. A successful bridge query only proves the session bridge answered; the subsequent watch-list transfer and game telemetry must also succeed. An unknown catalog hash is not interpreted as an offline session: the engine's watch-list result remains authoritative.
+
+The wire format follows [Caduceus's bridge implementation](https://github.com/Rian6/caduceus/blob/311f4eabc4ddaad649cbd0d60859f27d0ffcbbfc/electron/caduceus-ra-bridge.ts). Caduceus's bundled engine version can differ from the standalone version recommended above; protocol compatibility does not establish identical game coverage or timing. This new mode has host tests for discovery, malformed/offline replies and host selection; **Caduceus end-to-end console tracking remains unverified**. The Xerabora hardware results below apply only to the tested Xerabora configuration.
+
+Both modes retain the existing restrictions: Ember and ordinary/custom Neutrino launches do not load RiptOPL's telemetry core (USB POPStarter uses its own IOP reader), UDP storage owns the NIC and blocks menu RA queries **and clears loaded telemetry before an incompatible UDP-backed launch**, and SMB/HTTP never let RA drain the game's receive descriptors or poll its sockets. In those modes RA observes notices already received by the normal SMAP driver and uses the known host's ARP entry for transmission. Missing host/MAC disables telemetry rather than disturbing game I/O. For saved watch lists launched without a fresh peer discovery, SMB/HTTP passive telemetry uses an explicitly configured RA companion IP if provided, otherwise the existing verified peer or legacy SMB/HTTP game server fallback. **In passive SMB/HTTP mode a queued receive FIFO defers telemetry snapshots**, leaving the disc stream's pending receive traffic ahead of achievement traffic. The send path is still hardware-unverified and may still interfere during continuous streaming. Choosing Caduceus does not bypass these guards or add hardcore support.
+
+### Neutrino in the RA edition
+
+The full RetroAchievements kit includes the existing **[ORBIT v1.0.2 Neutrino runtime](https://github.com/danielnuld/ps2-launcher/releases/tag/v1.0.2)** by **Daniel / danielnuld**, based on Neutrino by **Maximus32** and the achievement protocol by **hacan359 / xeRAbora**. Its AFL-3.0 license, exact source commit and archive SHA-256 ship beside the runtime. Standard builds and install kits retain ordinary upstream Neutrino. An ELF-only update does not install the external runtime; copy the RA kit's complete `neutrino/` folder to your chosen supported device.
+
+The RA kit's `neutrino/` is ORBIT's **RA-Neutrino**, labelled by `neutrino/RA-EDITION-NEUTRINO.txt`. It keeps the usual folder name so RA setups find it, but it is **not** the standard kit's Neutrino: ORBIT v1.0.2 is built on **Neutrino v1.8.0**, so in the RA edition every Neutrino launch, tracked or not, uses that base and lacks later upstream changes, including Neutrino cheat support. Use the standard kit for Neutrino cheats. Keep each kit's `neutrino/` with its own loader rather than mixing them.
+
+Either RA mode works. With **RA Mode: Xerabora**, set **RA companion IP** to the signed-in xeRAbora PC. With **RA Mode: Caduceus**, the in-game agent reports to the engine on the Caduceus PC: Caduceus adds a session bridge, but its game telemetry is xeRAbora's wire protocol. Local-storage launches recheck the Caduceus session first and launch without achievements if it reports OFFLINE, exactly as OPL-core launches do. ORBIT is a loader runtime, so it does not need a third RA protocol mode; its agent matches xeRAbora's current wire protocol (unchanged since alpha.10, the version ORBIT copied is identical to alpha.17's). Run **RA: check game support** for a local image or APA install to identify that exact image and save `RA/<serial>.wl` under its support-file prefix. xeRAbora alpha.17 persists the serial-to-hash mapping in its own configuration; keep that mapping with the companion profile. A watch list copied from another PC does not identify the game to a fresh profile. Recheck support after replacing an image, especially another revision with the same serial.
+
+On launch, RiptOPL writes `ript-ra.wl` and `ript-ra.toml` into the selected Neutrino configuration directory, honoring its effective `-cwd` and chaining the user's `-cfg` and generated cheat configuration (the v1.8.0-based runtime has no cheat engine, so the codes do not apply). That directory must be writable and remain accessible to Neutrino. The runtime can reside on any supported mounted device; a raw `hdd0:` partition path is not a filesystem directory. RiptOPL adds the RA arguments only after preparation succeeds and validates the full launch argument budget before teardown. A game without a saved watch list launches without a notice. If the two RA arguments would push the launch past Neutrino's 256-byte budget, the game launches without achievements and shows the untracked notice instead of being refused.
+
+The pinned agent sends each snapshot in one packet, so a set whose **direct values exceed 1,216 bytes** cannot be tracked and launches without achievements, as it does under ORBIT. Sets with **pointer chains** are tracked the way ORBIT tracks them: the agent reads the direct entries and ignores the chain tail, as xeRAbora's protocol defines for an agent without chain support, so achievements that depend on a chain do not unlock on Neutrino launches while the rest of the set does. An unavailable runtime/companion address or failed configuration writes leave the launch untracked. **Console unlock cards/pulses and remote reset are not provided by this external runtime**; unlocks appear on the companion. Neutrino's own IGR behavior remains governed by its settings, independently of the OPL-core Mode 6 behavior above.
+
+UDPFS/UDPBD launches reuse the runtime's storage network stack; the adapter does not load a second SMAP/ministack or open menu RA sockets. These launches currently require a **static PS2 IP**, an explicit companion address (or a previously verified peer), and a saved watch list plus a matching serial/hash mapping already known by that companion. On **UDPFS** (Files and IMG) the support check now runs from the menu like on USB: RiptOPL sends its requests through the UDPFS network module itself, so the first check of a game no longer needs a local copy. The watch list is saved in `RA/` on the share, so PS2-Servers must not be started read-only. Classic **UDPBD** still cannot run the check; prepare it on a local copy of the **same image**. This is an integration candidate with host-test coverage, not current-build physical-console acceptance. ORBIT's published console results establish upstream evidence only. Test game I/O, telemetry, reconnects, IGR and both runtime locations before treating this handoff as release-validated.
+
+The ORBIT runtime watch uses the same schedule as the ordinary Neutrino watch: **05:41 UTC on the 1st, 11th and 21st of each month**. It opens or updates one compatibility-review issue when the release tag or archive digest changes. It does not automatically replace the reviewed pin.
+
+### Xerabora achievement browser
+
+In **RA Mode: Xerabora**, choose **RetroAchievements library (xeRAbora)** from the main menu, or **View achievements** on a game, to see the account's played games and each game's achievements, points, descriptions and unlock dates, with the same **L1/R1** pages, **Square** All / Earned / Locked / Hardcore filter and **Select** refresh as the Caduceus browser. The data comes from the xeRAbora PC's own interface (port **18280**), as in ORBIT: turn on **OPEN TO THE NETWORK** in xeRAbora's settings and allow `xerabora.exe` through the PC's firewall. RiptOPL finds the PC through the **RA companion IP**, a verified peer, or discovery.
+
+From a game card, RiptOPL hashes the image and looks the game up on retroachievements.org by that hash over HTTPS, exactly as ORBIT does, then shows its full list from xeRAbora, including a game you have **never played**. The lookup needs the console to reach the internet and its **clock to be set** (certificates are checked against it; RiptOPL trusts only six pinned root certificates and verifies the host name). Without internet, or with the clock unset, RiptOPL falls back to matching the title in your account library: a game you have never played then shows its title and achievement count with a note to play it once, and an unset clock is named on screen. xeRAbora reports unlock dates but not per-achievement hardcore status, so the Hardcore filter shows hardcore totals only in the library view. Achievement badge images are not downloaded in this mode.
+
+This browser needs TCP to reach xeRAbora's interface, which the UDPFS network module does not have, so it cannot open while **UDPFS** (Files or IMG) or **UDPBD** is the network protocol. The support check itself still works on UDPFS.
+
+### Caduceus achievement browser and in-game cards
+
+Choose **Caduceus achievements** from the main menu to browse account games and progress. Select a game to see its achievements, points, descriptions and unlock dates. **L1/R1** changes pages, **Square** cycles All / Earned / Locked / Hardcore, **Select** refreshes, and Back returns to the previous view. Hardcore is a filter for existing account history; it does not enable hardcore tracking.
+
+For a local ISO, **View achievements** in the game's menu hashes the image and opens that game's set directly. Formats that cannot be hashed on the console can still be browsed through the account library. The browser uses the I/O worker; Back remains available during network requests. Requests finish with bounded retries and cannot overwrite each other's shared state.
+
+With SMB or no network protocol selected, the browser requires a configured, accessible SMB share containing **ART/CADUCEUS.KEY**, created by the companion service, even when games are on USB, MMCE or internal exFAT storage. When **UDPFS Files** is the network protocol, no SMB share is needed: the browser reads the key and badges from `ART/` (or `art/`) in the folder PS2-Servers shares instead, and sends its requests through the UDPFS network module; point the companion's key at that folder and set the **RA companion IP**. UDPFS IMG and UDPBD cannot open the browser. If UDPFS is selected but its page has not been opened since the console started, open it once so the UDPFS network module loads; the support check and the link test say so too. The key is a 64-character pairing capability, not an RA password/API key. The service also supplies optional **ART/<icon-key>_RA.png** badge images; only the visible page is cached. Permit UDP **18198** for browser queries. Pairing requests go only to the explicit **RA companion IP**, or to the configured SMB server IPv4 address when that setting is **0.0.0.0**, never to LAN broadcast. Restrict the share to trusted devices: the LAN protocol is unencrypted. To revoke pairing, stop the service, remove its key file and restart it.
+
+Caduceus unlock notices use the existing gold pulse by default. Its experimental card renderer can display a compact upper-right achievement title and points when explicitly enabled; older notices without a title show the ID. Duplicate notices are suppressed, while a richer title-bearing copy can upgrade a legacy notice. Xerabora retains its gold pulse. The new card and browser are adapted from [Rian6's PS2 implementation](https://github.com/Rian6/caduceus-opl/tree/2a98a9d7361f8e33f421244bc88c318eab3bdf2f), under AFL-3.0.
+
+**Caduceus experimental cards defaults to Off.** In the RA build, open **Settings > Network**, select **RA Mode: Caduceus**, enable telemetry, and enable **Caduceus experimental cards** only if you accept possible flicker or game freezes. Save settings and launch a tracked PS2 game with the OPL core. The choice takes effect on the next launch and is saved as `ra_experimental_cards=1`; old configurations without that key remain Off. Xerabora always uses the gold pulse, and POPStarter PS1 unlocks remain companion-only.
+
+This is the upstream-style IMAGE renderer with additional event and GIF-state checks, not a proven safe GS ownership scheme. An idle GIF/DMA check or observed `DISPFB` write cannot guarantee that the game has finished or preserved a pending image transfer. If a game flickers or freezes with cards enabled, disable the setting before relaunching. The setting help also states this risk.
+
+With cards Off, launches reserve no card workspace and install no extra GS tracker. An opted-in tracked Caduceus launch reserves about 30 KiB in protected module storage and observes display-register writes without forcing a video mode. If there is insufficient room, unlock and reset events fall back to the existing pulse handler. The renderer skips blocked GIF states and unsupported framebuffers, but these guards do not prove graphics compatibility; a card may not appear for an unsupported display. Current-build console card rendering and real unlock acceptance remain unverified. The existing network-share stability warning still applies.
