+# VMC creation and cancelled-launch audit ? 2026-10-08
+
+Source baseline: Open-PS2-Loader `rebuild/main` at 9ef8d643a739d88a4f4b5fd055a209d0b27299c4. No console reproduction was performed.
+
+## Cancelled native launch: already fixed
+
+Commit afa0ab42 introduced `sbMcemuSlotWord` and per-image slot caches in BDM, SMB, HDD and MMCE. Each image is searched once, within size/4 words. Subsequent launches reuse the cached offsets after settings replace the markers. Every loop rewrites both slots, including inactive slots; HDD explicitly clears an unassigned slot. No marker restoration is needed with this design. Production-helper and call-site regression tests pass, along with the separate cdvdman patch-zone restoration tests. Do not reintroduce a second marker restoration mechanism.
+
+## Creation and allocation
+
+`sysCheckVMC` starts genvmc for a missing or different-sized card. genvmc formats it through sequential block writes, closes it, and reports status. It does not reserve a contiguous extent or defragment the volume. A fresh file and ample free space do not guarantee contiguous allocation. Recreating a saved card can destroy its contents; recovery requires a backup.
+
+The completion path previously used CHECK_CHAIN, whose BDM contract reduces every count other than one to zero. This patch uses GET_FRAGLIST's count-only query after the existing GET_LBA sentinel guard: one means contiguous, more than one means fragmented, and zero/negative is unknown. This distinguishes empty/failed queries from proven fragmentation without disabling native launch checks. It is a diagnosis correction, not a proven repair of the reported allocation failures.
+
+Primary contract: https://github.com/ps2dev/ps2sdk/blob/master/iop/fs/bdmfs_fatfs/src/fs_driver.c (`get_frag_list`, `fs_ioctl2`). The native BDM game path already uses GET_FRAGLIST; no fragment-buffer allocation is required for a count-only call.
+
+## Different runtime limits
+
+Native BDM mcemu writes a contiguous sector range and retains CHECK_CHAIN and the 32-bit LBA range guard at launch. Native APA/PFS mcemu uses bounded inode/block mapping and can reject cards it cannot map. MMCE has a different file-backed contract; unsupported completion ioctls must remain unknown.
+
+The bundled Neutrino revision 7be8de2 maps BDM files with multiple extents and shares a 64-fragment budget across ISO and VMC files (`ee/loader/src/fhi_config.c`). RiptOPL's native contiguity warning is not proof that Neutrino cannot use the file. Its APA path intentionally omits VMC arguments because the bundled core lacks the PFS backing implementation.
+
+## Remaining hardware evidence
+
+User reports USB/MMCE creation warnings and issue #830 describes internal exFAT/Neutrino failures changing after recreation and reset. The MMCE unsupported-query bug was fixed in #877. The current evidence does not prove a common cause for USB and internal exFAT. Needed: exact build, filesystem, selected core, error text and stage, card size/path, resulting fragment count, and whether the unchanged file fails after reboot. Do not relax write safety checks or claim issue #830 fixed on host results.
+
+## Validation
+
+Host tests: test_vmc_contiguity.py, test_auto_create_vmc.py, test_mcemu_slots.py and test_patch_zone_restore.py. The completion test executes production code with contiguous, multi-fragment, zero, negative, unsupported-MMCE and failed-open cases. Console allocation/save/reboot behavior remains unverified.
+
+## Follow-up: creation recovery and core-aware reporting
+
+At baseline 7452341a, mc_writecluster returned after a failed or short write without releasing genvmc_abort_sema. The next creation worker and abort operation wait on that semaphore, so one storage failure can poison the remaining session. The fix releases it on the write-error exit; a production-function harness verifies failed writes, short writes, successful retries and cancellation preserve the token. This is a confirmed source defect, not proof of issue #830's reported fragmentation cause.
+
+Both manual and automatic creation warnings now respect the effective per-game core, including Default following the global setting. Neutrino does not inherit native mcemu's single-extent warning. Native launch-time contiguity and LBA safety checks remain in place; Neutrino still applies its own shared extent budget. This does not reserve contiguous space or repair fragmented storage.
+
+## Follow-up: VMC menu core resolution and Neutrino extent overflow prompt
+
+1. In the VMC configuration dialog (`guiGameShowVMCConfig` and `guiGameShowVMCMenu`), `coreLoader` previously evaluated against an uninitialized static variable if the Compatibility dialog had not been opened in the same session. The dialog now resolves the effective launch core via `guiGameResolveEffectiveCore`, ensuring games defaulting to Neutrino or explicitly configured for it suppress the native single-extent fragmentation warning on creation.
+2. At launch time (`bdmNeutrinoFragBudgetOk`), if the combined fragment count of the ISO and VMCs exceeds the 64-extent limit, but the ISO itself fits within 64 extents, RiptOPL now prompts the user whether to continue using the physical memory card in that slot instead of silently falling through to the native OPL core (which would fail immediately under mcemu's single-extent check). If the user accepts, the offending VMC is dropped and the title launches under Neutrino; if the user declines, the launch aborts cleanly to the menu.
+
